From 8113c0c4df3ad05f633448578525a3ea48760d83 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 20:05:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Performance/correctness audit: fix silent report-list
 truncation, add composite index on reports

Went through the repo looking for algorithmic/structural improvements.
Most of the obvious wins were already done (batched author/reviewer
name lookups instead of N+1, in-memory + HTTP caching on the country
data endpoints, memoized derived data in the Observatory charts) - the
two real findings below are what's actually left.

Silent report-list truncation (client/src/pages/*.jsx):
GET /api/reports, /api/reports/pending, and /api/reports/mine are all
capped at pagination.DEFAULT_PAGE_SIZE (100) unless the caller passes
?limit=. Four pages call these with no limit at all, expecting to get
everything back in one response, since none of them have any
pagination UI of their own:
  - ReportsBrowse.jsx groups/counts all published reports by category
    client-side for instant tab-switching
  - Reports.jsx just wanted a total count for its "X reports so far"
    copy, but was computing it from the (capped) response body itself
  - PeerReview.jsx's pending-review queue and PeerReviewMine.jsx's "my
    submissions" list are both rendered in full, no pager
This isn't a problem yet at current content volume, but it's a
ticking correctness bug: the moment any of these lists passes 100
rows, results silently go wrong with no error and no visible sign
anything is missing - undercounted "X reports so far", missing older
reports from category browsing, an incomplete review queue.

- ReportsBrowse.jsx, PeerReview.jsx, PeerReviewMine.jsx: request
  ?limit=200 explicitly (MAX_PAGE_SIZE, this endpoint's actual
  ceiling) instead of the silent 100-row default. Documented that
  past 200 rows each of these needs real pagination, which none of
  them have today - this fixes the immediate silent-failure risk, not
  the underlying "these pages assume unbounded lists" design.
- Reports.jsx: switched from data.length to reading the X-Total-Count
  response header (already set by paginated_json_response() for
  exactly this purpose), and requests ?limit=1 since the response
  body itself is unused - correct regardless of how many published
  reports exist, and trims that fetch's body from up to 100 report
  objects down to 1.

Composite index on reports (review_status, status)
(server/models/report.py, new migration):
get_published_reports(), get_pending_reports(), and
get_changes_requested_reports() all filter on both columns together.
Each already had its own single-column index, but a two-column
WHERE clause can only make full use of one single-column index at a
time (SQLite has no automatic index merge; Postgres falls back to a
less efficient bitmap merge) - the other column's matches still get
filtered by scanning/re-checking rows one at a time. Added
ix_reports_review_status_status so the database can satisfy the whole
WHERE clause with a single index seek instead - confirmed with
EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN (SEARCH ... USING INDEX ix_reports_review_status_status,
not a table scan).

Declared as __table_args__ on the model AND as a migration, not just
one or the other - verified both Base.metadata.create_all() and
`alembic upgrade head` produce the byte-identical index set. Keeping
these in sync is exactly the lesson from the earlier reports-table
schema-drift incident (see fix_reports_schema.py / docs/DEPLOYMENT.md)
- an index that only exists on one of the two bootstrap paths is the
same kind of silent split-brain schema that caused that one.

Checked but left alone (no clear win, or too large a change for what
was found):
- The two >500kB build chunks vite's own build output warns about
  (main vendor chunk, Observatory's chunk) - Observatory being large
  is inherent to it being an isolated, already-lazy-loaded chunk for
  a genuinely heavy globe/charting feature, not loaded on any other
  page. The main chunk is Rollup's automatic shared-vendor grouping,
  not an eager heavy import anywhere in App.jsx - untangling it
  further needs bundle-visualizer tooling to do responsibly rather
  than guessed manualChunks config.
- image_processing.py (fellow photo normalization) is already tight:
  fixed 600x600 JPEG, quality 85, optimize=True.
- The exact same unbounded-list assumption likely exists on the
  admin/reviewer side (e.g. GET /api/reports/all backing
  ReportCategoryControl.jsx, and whatever backs
  PeerReviewDeletions.jsx/DeletedReportsPage.jsx on main) - not
  checked in this pass since this branch (database) doesn't have that
  code yet; worth the same treatment once it lands here.
